The thesis is strong. Let me be critical about what you're missing:
What's right:
- ChatGPT owns discovery, you own fulfillment — correct framing
- The supplier graph is genuinely under-owned
- Image → product → one-click buy is a real workflow gap
- "We normalize bad suppliers" is the actual value prop
What you're missing:
1. The Trust Layer is the real moat, not the infrastructure.
Anyone can build a supplier OS. The hard part is:
- Certifying suppliers (insurance, qualifications, reviews)
- Providing guarantees (if Jim doesn't show up, you refund)
- Building reputation scores from real transactions
That's what makes customers come back. Not the software.
2. You're thinking two-sided market but it's actually B2B2C.
The supplier IS your customer. They pay you. The end user gets a free experience. This changes everything:
- You don't compete with ChatGPT for consumers
- You sell to suppliers: "Get more bookings for £X/month"
- Suppliers are your distribution channel
3. The dropshipping play is actually two businesses glued together.
- Business A: Image recognition → product matching (tech)
- Business B: Supplier management (ops)
Business A is a feature. Business B is the company. Don't confuse them.
4. What you're not saying but should be:
- The supplier data flywheel: more suppliers → better matching → more customers → more suppliers
- The switching cost: once Jim's availability is in your system, he can't leave without losing bookings
- The enterprise version: white-label this for比较大 companies with field service teams
5. Additional opportunities you're missing:
a) The "Quote as a Service" play
- Customer describes what they need
- Your system generates a real quote (not a ballpark)
- Supplier confirms or adjusts
- Customer pays deposit
- This is worth 10x more than just a booking
b) The "Last Mile" play
- ChatGPT sends customer to website
- Website has broken forms, slow load, no mobile
- Your system: "ChatGPT sent you here. Want me to book this for you?"
- You're the bridge between discovery and transaction
c) The "Supplier Intelligence" play
- Jim doesn't know his utilization is 67%
- You tell him: "Book more Thursday afternoons, you're empty then"
- You tell him: "Customers who book EV chargers also need a fuse box upgrade"
- This is the data advantage — you know things about his business he doesn't
d) The "Language Expansion" play
- UK first (English)
- Then: Polish, Romanian, Hindi (big UK trade populations)
- Voice agents in 5 languages = 5x market
- Each language is a separate product line
e) The "Insurance/Warranty" play
- "Book through us and get a 12-month guarantee"
- This is the trust layer
- Suppliers pay extra for this, customers demand it
f) The "Enterprise" play
- White-label your supplier OS for比较大 companies
- "We manage your field service team's scheduling, pricing, and customer comms"
- This is the B2B revenue stream
The sharpest version of your thesis:
We're not building a marketplace. We're building the operating system for service businesses.
- ChatGPT: "Here are your options"
- Google: "Here are your options"
- You: "Done. Booked. Paid. Guaranteed."
